Audit this circuit for vulnerabilities:

pragma circom 2.0.0;

template RangeCheckTemplate() {
   signal input in;
   signal bits[8];

   for (var i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
       bits[i] <-- (in >> i) & 1;
   }
}

Vulnerability: Missing range constraint (unconstrained bit decomposition).

Description:
The circuit assigns each bits[i] with <-- from (in >> i) & 1. That is witness computation only; it does not add R1CS constraints by itself. There are no constraints forcing each bit to be 0 or 1, and no constraint that reconstructs in from the bits.

Impact:
A prover can pick arbitrary bit values (or leave them unconstrained) while choosing a large in. The verifier gets no proof that in fits in 0..255 or that the bits match in, which breaks range assumptions and can enable overflow-style attacks at the protocol level.

Fix:
Constrain each bit as boolean and add a reconstruction constraint so the weighted sum of bits equals in.

Corrected code:
pragma circom 2.0.0;

template RangeCheckTemplate() {
   signal input in;
   signal bits[8];
   var sum = 0;

   for (var i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
       bits[i] <-- (in >> i) & 1;
       bits[i] * (bits[i] - 1) === 0;
       sum += bits[i] * (2 ** i);
   }
   in === sum;
}

